# Normalize line endings on checkout so byte-sensitive consumers are stable
# across Linux / macOS / Windows clones:
#   - scripts/distribute.py:compute_source_hash (AGENTS.md drift detector)
#   - tests/golden/* and the DL3c golden snapshot + .sha256 integrity check
# Without this, git autocrlf=true on Windows would check these out as CRLF,
# changing their bytes and spuriously failing `distribute check` / golden CI.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.py   text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.cfg  text eol=lf
*.sha256 text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# --- Distribution curation (consumed by `git archive`, i.e. scripts/publish.py) ---
# Dev/maintenance-only artifacts are EXCLUDED from the published snapshot via
# `export-ignore` (git archive honors it). The dev repo keeps them; only the
# end-user product is slimmed. End-users get the skills + runtime + a
# user-facing guide (CLAUDE.md via dev-only marker strip; README.md / Makefile
# via .dist swap — see publish.py), not the test suite, design docs, CI, or
# empty spec dirs.
tests/          export-ignore
docs/           export-ignore
schema/         export-ignore
.github/        export-ignore
.githooks/      export-ignore
.replay-accepted-drift-dl4.yaml export-ignore

# Dev/maintenance/publish-only scripts (no runtime importer — verified). Kept
# for the maintainer; end-users never run publish/release/audit/test/dev tools.
# (scripts/distribute.py = setup and scripts/update.py = updates are KEPT.)
scripts/publish.py            export-ignore
scripts/release.py            export-ignore
scripts/audit_fail_open.py    export-ignore
scripts/smoke_test.py         export-ignore
scripts/replay_and_diff.py    export-ignore
scripts/check_artifact_compat.py export-ignore
scripts/dev/    export-ignore

# Dev-authoring rule — auto-loaded every session by Claude Code but useless to an
# end-user who only RUNS the skills (it governs HOW to write scripts: pathlib,
# sys.executable, no shell=True, UTF-8 file I/O). ZERO runtime reference — the
# analysis never reads it; its CLAUDE.md mention is inside the dev-only fence
# (also stripped). publish's bake regenerates AGENTS.md over the stripped tree, so
# the source_hash stays consistent. NOTE: skill-architecture.md / producer-consumer.md
# are deliberately KEPT — their `#N` clauses are cited as provenance by runtime
# prompts + SKILL.md (unknown≠zero, subagent heredoc .md-write, atomic vocab update);
# stripping them would leave dangling citations in the shipped files.
.claude/rules/development.md  export-ignore

# The operator's own trading record. Committed to the dev repo, and PUSHED, as
# its off-site backup (SKILL.md's commit-and-push protocol) — a commit that
# never leaves this machine is not a backup, since it lives in the same clone
# as the data it protects and a lost machine loses both (F4, external review).
# Never published. publish.py's guard_private_paths aborts if either survives.
reports/track-record/ export-ignore
trade-journal.jsonl    export-ignore

# The track-record ORCHESTRATION layer stays private; the RUNTIME ships.
#
# The skill is what binds this tool to one operator. Its commit-and-push
# protocol pushes with no refspec to whatever `origin` the clone has — and a
# marketplace recipient's origin is the PUBLIC org repo, with the published
# .gitignore's `!reports/track-record/` making the broker archive and the
# intent journal committable out of the box. A recipient who points origin at
# their own public fork to make the backup work would publish their trade
# history. `publish.py`'s private-path guard runs on the MAINTAINER's snapshot
# and is inert on their machine. Until a backup topology exists that is
# correct for a stranger, the orchestration layer does not ship. Step 1's
# hard-STOP on the (export-ignored) freeze file is the deterministic second
# gate that keeps it that way.
#
# `scripts/track_record/` DOES ship: it is a verbatim archiver with
# fail-closed coverage gates, an append-only journal with whole-file topology
# validation, and a quarterly renderer whose every number degrades to
# `unavailable (reason)` rather than guess. It runs no git, takes no
# orchestration decisions, and depends only on stdlib plus
# `scripts.sources.yfinance_guard`. Its own README states what it is not.
.claude/skills/track-record/ export-ignore
